    {
      "number": 818,
      "title": "worktrees: WaveId identity, sibling/child verbs, and stack navigation",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op wt create my-feature              # sibling: root branch from main (the default)\nlf op wt create fix-auth --child        # child of the current branch → parent.fix-auth\nlf op wt up                             # cd to the parent worktree in the stack\nlf op wt down fix-auth                  # cd to a child by leaf\nlf op wt list                           # worktrees as an indented tree\nlf op wt switch fix-auth                # switch by wave name, chain leaf, or full branch\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReplaces the configurable `branch_names` schema with a fixed `WaveId` identity type — one source of truth for how a unit of work names itself, with two deliberately non-derivable projections: a flat, author-free `dir_component` (`bugs.fix-auth.20260706_0801`) for the local worktree path and an author-scoped `branch` (`jack/bugs.fix-auth.20260706_0801`) for the remote. The chain encodes lineage as a hint; the authoritative parent/child DAG lives on the `Run` record and is never parsed back out of a name. Worktree creation collapses to two relative-to-here verbs — **sibling** (the default, roots an independent branch from main) and **child** (stacks under its parent) — and `lf op wt` gains stack-aware `up`/`down` navigation and a tree-view `list`. Landing no longer rotates the worktree; instead lfd prunes a worker's tree when its remote branch is deleted.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `engine/identity.rs`: `WaveId` + `Timestamp` with a single liberal `parse` funnel and strict projections; retires `BranchNameConfig` and the `branch_names` config knob.\n- `lf op wt create` reworked to `--sibling`/`--child` (dropping `--stack`/`--main`/`--fork`); adds `up`/`down` stack navigation and a stack-aware tree `list`; `switch` resolves by wave name, chain leaf, or full branch.\n- `lf op land` / `submit` no longer rotate the worktree; the wave home stays put and a merged worker's tree is pruned when its branch is deleted (new lfd hook path).\n- Docs (`docs/config.md`, `docs/lfop.md`, builtin `LOOPFLOW.md`, step prompts), goldens, and DATAMODEL notes updated to the new vocabulary and behavior.\n- Tests updated across worktree, land, next, rebase, and config suites; `land_tests` asserts submit never merges.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1519,
      "deletions": 1465,
      "changed_files": 40
    },
    {
      "number": 819,
      "title": "lf op release: make scheduled notes work without agent CLIs",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncargo test -p loopflow release_notes --test release_tests\n```\n\nThe new test stubs `lf --batch release-notes` to fail with a missing Claude CLI error. `lf op release notes` detects the missing-agent failure, falls back to deterministic notes, folds in `release/unreleased/DECISIONS.md`, writes `RELEASE_NOTES.md`, archives `release/v<version>/NOTES.md`, and clears `release/unreleased/`.\n\n## Summary\n\nWeekly scheduled patch releases shouldn't depend on a human-installed Claude, Codex, or OpenCode CLI on the GitHub runner. `run_release_notes_step` still tries the agent-backed `release-notes` step first; when that step fails specifically because an agent binary can't be spawned, it now falls back to the existing mechanical note generator instead of failing the release. The fallback reuses the same release context — version, previous tag, target, merged PRs, and archived decisions — so the archive contract and intent source stay identical to the interactive path.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `run_release_notes_step` catches missing-agent-CLI errors and generates deterministic notes rather than failing.\n- `is_missing_release_notes_agent_cli` narrowly matches errors that name an agent CLI *and* a missing-binary/spawn condition; all other `release-notes` failures still fail the release.\n- `generate_release_notes` now takes optional release decisions and emits a `## Release decisions` section, keeping the CI path's intent source aligned with the agent path.\n- Docs in `docs/lfop.md` and `release/README.md` note that scheduled release automation no longer requires a runner-local agent CLI.\n- New `release_notes_falls_back_when_agent_cli_is_missing` integration test covers the full fallback: mechanical notes, decisions carried through, PR titles included, `NOTES.md` archived, `release/unreleased/` removed.\n\nNot included: this does not install an agent CLI in CI or add model credentials to the weekly workflow.\n\n## Validation\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy -p loopflow -- -D warnings\ncargo test -p loopflow --test release_tests\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all\n```\n\n`scripts/test.py --all` passed Python, Rust, website, Swift, and e2e. The local Concerto UI runner failed before bootstrapping with `Early unexpected exit ... signal kill before establishing connection`; no test assertion failed.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 91,
      "deletions": 10,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 820,
      "title": "git: supply a committer identity when the environment has none",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\n# On a headless host with no user.name/user.email (CI runner, cron Mac):\ncargo test -p loopflow commit_supplies_fallback_identity_when_none_configured\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nHeadless hosts — GitHub Actions runners and the self-hosted cron Mac — have no `user.name`/`user.email` configured, so `git commit` refuses with \"empty ident name\" and automated commits (e.g. the release version bump) fail. This teaches `commit()` to detect a missing identity and supply a `loopflow` fallback committer via `-c user.name`/`-c user.email`. A developer's configured identity is never overridden — only a truly absent one (empty output or non-zero exit) triggers the fallback.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `has_git_identity()` checks for both `user.name` and `user.email`; empty or failing lookups count as absent.\n- `commit()` injects `loopflow <loopflow@users.noreply.github.com>` only when no identity is configured.\n- Test isolates from global/system git config (`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`/`GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM=/dev/null`) to exercise the no-identity path deterministically and asserts the fallback author lands on the commit.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 71,
      "deletions": 1,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 821,
      "title": "lfdb: sweep three pieces of confirmed-dead code",
      "body": "## What changed\n\nRemoves three confirmed-dead pieces of code and adds a migration to drop the tables behind them.\n\n- **`secrets_provider_config` table** (from migration 031) had zero references anywhere in `src/` — nothing ever read or wrote it.\n- **`wave_pr_merge_events` table** (from 009, recreated in 011) was insert-only via `record_merge_event`, with no `SELECT` anywhere; the writer and its only callers lived in test code. The writer, the `record_merge_event` methods on `Store`/`SqliteStore`/`WaveStateStore`, and the `QueueMergeEvent` DTO go with it.\n- **`worker_dispatched`/`worker_finished` serde aliases** on the journal's `RunObserved`/`RunCompleted` event kinds — the pre-rename compatibility shim is no longer needed, so the aliases and the replay-equivalence test are dropped. The test is replaced by a leaner one asserting new appends serialize under the post-rename names.\n\n## Migration\n\n```sql\n-- 051_drop_dead_tables.sql\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS secrets_provider_config;\nDROP TABLE IF EXISTS wave_pr_merge_events;\n```\n\n`IF EXISTS` keeps the drops convergent across every db history.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 28,
      "deletions": 125,
      "changed_files": 7
    },
    {
      "number": 822,
      "title": "wave/goals: reconcile architecture-direction with the merged tree",
      "body": "## What this is\n\nDocs-only update to `wave/goals/` — the durable architecture record for the wave-agent work, re-grounded against the actual merged tree.\n\n```bash\ngit diff main -- wave/goals/architecture-direction.md\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe architecture-direction doc had drifted ahead of the code: it read as a long staging-debt narrative for work that was, in fact, already done. This PR reconciles it against a top-down four-layer audit of the merged tree (wave core, lfdb substrate, engine+harness, lfd+lf+DTOs). The headline is that the tree is *truer than the doc was* — roughly 80% of the M1/M2 debt list only survived on paper. Resolved items are now marked `[done]` with the PR that closed them, and a new \"Current state (2026-07-06 audit)\" section becomes the ground-truth map the next full review should start from.\n\nTwo direction calls are ratified in the process:\n\n- **The lfd dissolution [R].** `lfd` stops being a fat HTTP API. Read routes become `lf` queries, hand routes are *removed* (Concerto → SSH / `lf`), crons/mind/journal belong to the per-wave server, and `lfd`-the-proxy shrinks to `/exec` + liveness + webhook ingress. This reframes what M1/M2 are now about.\n- **Live telemetry [R].** Drop the machine-level aggregate `/ws`; base contract is per-wave SSE `/events` + `lf` queries for discovery/status. Aggregation returns later as a performance proxy in the hosted deployment, not the base.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New \"Current state\" audit section with a four-tier map of \"unnecessary\" (already-deleted, genuinely-dead-being-cut, the one real charter gap = lfd is still a hand, and shape questions deferred to the full review).\n- Anatomy rewritten: `lf` is hands *and* query surface; the per-wave reactive server is split out from what used to be lumped as \"lfd\"; `lfd` marked dissolving.\n- M0 marked done (#796 reactive server); M1/M2 work-lists reconciled with verified `[done]` status per item; remaining work refocused on the dissolution.\n- Ratified: a wave targets exactly one repo (the `Wave { repos: [RepoWork] }` / `wave_repos` multi-repo model is being stripped); immediate-kill interrupts accepted over the cooperative→grace→kill design.\n- `wave-agent-follow-ups.md` updated to match.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 254,
      "deletions": 57,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 823,
      "title": "wave memory: split the add stream from the compiled checkpoint",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf memory                                   # print the wave's compiled MEMORY.md\nlf memory add \"buttons: variants unified\"   # publish one replayable fact to the stream\nlf memory update < MEMORY.md                # replace the compiled checkpoint from stdin\nlf sub                                      # follow the wave — turns, state, memory adds\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf memory add` used to append a raw bullet onto the wave's `MEMORY.md`, so every fact accreted into the compiled file forever. This splits the two: `add` now publishes a fact to a replayable memory stream without mutating the checkpoint, while `update` remains the only writer of the compiled `MEMORY.md`. The stream carries full added facts since the last externalization; `update` writes a fresh checkpoint and clears the accumulated delta, since the checkpoint becomes the new seed. This keeps the compiled memory a curated artifact rather than an ever-growing bullet log, while still letting subscribers replay what's been added since the last fold.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `MemoryAdded { fact }` journal event, produced by the `add` memory route; `MemoryUpdated` now clears the replayable add delta on checkpoint.\n- `wave` runtime gains `append_memory`, the publish path for stream facts (distinct from `update_memory`, which writes the file).\n- New `memory-add` SSE event on `/events`: full added facts replay on subscribe then arrive live; `memory` stays curation summaries. `lf sub` renders these as `memory added: ...`.\n- Reworked memory tests: `update` writes the origin file and journals `MemoryUpdated`; `add` journals `MemoryAdded` without touching the compiled file.\n- Docs updated across `docs/lf.md`, the wave README, and the `/events` / `/memory` route tables to describe the checkpoint-vs-stream split.\n- Seeds a `wave/memory` GOAL.md and MEMORY.md for the memory-stream wave.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 405,
      "deletions": 121,
      "changed_files": 13
    },
    {
      "number": 824,
      "title": "wave: collapse the wave model from multi-repo to single-repo",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nuv run pytest python/tests/\ncargo test\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA wave now targets exactly one repo, so the multi-repo indirection is gone. The old `RepoWork`/`RepoWorkDto` wrapper (a `Vec` of per-repo execution surfaces) is removed and its fields — repo, iteration, status, worktree/branch, commits, PR + git snapshot — are carried inline on the `Wave`/`WaveDto` across all three wire mirrors (Rust, Python, Swift). This kills the split-brain of a \"list of repos\" that in practice always held one element, and simplifies every consumer that used to reach through `wave.repos.first()` or iterate the vec.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Rust: flatten `RepoWork` into `Wave` and `RepoWorkDto` into `WaveDto`; update executor, bridge, queue, catalog, sqlite, and route handlers (attention/hooks/repos/waves) to read the inline fields directly.\n- SQLite migration `052_wave_single_repo.sql` collapses per-repo rows onto the wave row.\n- Python: merge `RepoWork` into the `Wave` pydantic model; update fixtures and contract/model tests.\n- Swift: inline the repo execution fields on `Wave` and update `WaveViewModel`, `LocalWaveService`, and Concerto state/tests.\n- DTO fixtures and contract tests updated; `repo` is now the required field (previously `repos`).\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 538,
      "deletions": 939,
      "changed_files": 38
    },
    {
      "number": 825,
      "title": "lfd: add POST /v0/exec — the generic lf-argv exec door (additive)",
      "body": "First increment of the lfd dissolution: a single generic exec door, additive — nothing deleted, no Concerto/Swift touched.\n\n`POST /v0/exec`, token-gated (rides the existing `/v0` bearer nest):\n- **`crate::lfd::lf_exec`** — host-neutral, state-free so the wave listener can reuse it verbatim: `validate_lf_argv` (clap `try_parse` gate — refuses empty/garbage before any spawn, the \"does it compile\" check) and `exec_lf` (no shell — `Command::args` — captures exit/stdout/stderr).\n- **`routes/exec.rs`** — `ExecRequest { argv, cwd }` → `ExecResponse { exit_code, stdout, stderr }`. Refused argv = 400; a non-zero `lf` exit is a successful door call reporting a failed run = 200.\n\nDiscovery worth recording: `lfq` does not exist yet (the client is unbuilt); `resolve_lf_binary()` + `Cli::try_parse_from` were the ready-made pieces.\n\nVerify: `cargo build`/`clippy --all-targets -D warnings`/`fmt` clean; 7 tests pass (parse-gate valid/unknown-flag/empty; route invalid/empty/valid-execs; **exec_requires_bearer_token** — no token → 401, token+garbage → 400).\n\nFollow-up (scoped in `scratch/lfd-exec.md`, NOT built): the wave-server `/exec` door (the actual in-wave sandbox-escape path) is gated on a subagent-auth decision — a worker subagent is a different principal than the resident mind and doesn't hold the resident token.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2556,
      "deletions": 3736,
      "changed_files": 64
    },
    {
      "number": 827,
      "title": "doctor: declare system dependencies (add uv, doppler) + Brewfile",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op doctor                     # check required + optional deps\nlf op doctor --brewfile > Brewfile   # regenerate the Brewfile\nbrew bundle                      # install everything\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf op doctor` now drives off a single declared `SYSTEM_DEPS` list instead of a hand-written per-tool `if which(...)` ladder. The list is the source of truth for both the doctor check and the repo-root `Brewfile`, which is generated (never hand-edited) via `lf op doctor --brewfile`. The check now distinguishes required build/run essentials from optional agent CLIs and editors, and reports a count of missing required deps at the end. Two new required deps are declared — `uv` (Python toolchain) and `doppler` (secrets) — alongside the existing git, rust, tmux, and gh.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Add `SystemDep`/`Brew` types and a declarative `SYSTEM_DEPS` array; doctor loops over it, honoring `macos_only` and required/optional tags with per-host install hints (brew on macOS, fallback URL/command elsewhere).\n- Add hidden `--brewfile` flag to `lf op doctor` that renders the Brewfile from the same list; commit the generated `Brewfile` at the repo root.\n- Add `uv` and `doppler` as required deps; drop the old `check_claude_available`/`check_codex_available`/`check_gemini_available` helpers.\n- Add tests asserting the declared list is well-formed and that the committed `Brewfile` matches the generated output (fails if stale).",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 257,
      "deletions": 60,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 828,
      "title": "lf op submit: a human-gated finish between pr and land",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op submit    # prep to land, mark ready, assign to you — you click merge\nlf op land      # hands-off: same prep, then arm auto-merge + rotate worktree\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lf op submit`, a middle gear between `lf op pr` and `lf op land`. It rebases onto main, clears `scratch/`, marks the PR ready, and assigns it to you — then stops. Nothing merges until you click merge on GitHub; that one click is the required gate. No auto-merge, no worktree rotation.\n\n`land` and `submit` share a single `prepare_pr()` and diverge only at the finalize step — `land` arms auto-merge and rotates the worktree, `submit` assigns the PR for review. `land`'s behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf op submit` command + `submit()` op, sharing `prepare_pr()` with `land()`\n- `submit.md` step builtin (agents can be dispatched to submit)\n- `pr` vs `submit` vs `land` guide in LOOPFLOW.md\n- Tests: submit assigns the reviewer, skips auto-merge, and doesn't rotate",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 539,
      "deletions": 21,
      "changed_files": 16
    },
    {
      "number": 829,
      "title": "release: v0.10.1",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op release run  # runs the canonical release flow\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nBumps version to v0.10.1 across Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml, and lockfiles. Adds release notes documenting the Linear PM migration, `lf op submit` command, headless reliability fixes, and system dependency declarations.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Version bump: 0.10.0 → 0.10.1 in Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml, Cargo.lock\n- New release notes in RELEASE_NOTES.md and release/v0.10.1/NOTES.md\n- uv.lock refreshed with updated dependency markers",
