    {
      "number": 830,
      "title": "op sync-skills: compile steps into home only, drop repo targets",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op sync-skills          # compile steps into ~/.claude/skills + ~/.agents/skills\nlf op sync-skills --yes    # skip the confirmation prompt\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf op sync-skills` now compiles resolved steps (builtins + your `~/.lf/`) exclusively into your personal home vendor Skill directories (`~/.claude/skills`, `~/.agents/skills`). It never writes into a working repo. This removes the split-brain between repo-local and global syncing: there's one destination and one behavior, so `/step` and `$step` invocations resolve the same skills regardless of which repo you're in.\n\nPreviously the command wrote repo-local skills by default and needed `--global` to reach home. That mode is gone, along with the machinery that made it safe inside a repo — the `.git/info/exclude` bookkeeping that hid generated `.claude/skills/` and `.agents/skills/` dirs from git.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `sync_skills` drops the `repo_root` argument and the `include_global` option; home is the sole target.\n- Removed `ensure_repo_skill_excludes`, `collect_repo_steps`, and repo-local resolution.\n- `--global` flag removed; `--yes` now confirms the (always home) write. Non-terminal runs still require `--yes`.\n- Tests reworked to assert everything lands under home and nothing escapes it.\n- README updated to describe home-only compilation.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 87,
      "deletions": 181,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 831,
      "title": "lf ssh: forward local credentials to a remote host (ssh_with_credits)",
      "body": "## What\n\n`lf ssh <host> [--repo <rel>] [--secret <NAME>]... [--forward-agent] -- <cmd...>` — an ssh transport that carries the caller's *local* credentials into a remote command, per invocation. This is not \"lf running a remote command\"; it's ssh gaining credential-forwarding so a stateless remote home can run `lf op …` authenticated as the user while storing nothing.\n\nConcerto (and the CLI) switch `ssh mini-heart lf op …` → `lf ssh mini-heart -- lf op …`.\n\n## Credential resolution (all local)\n\n| Credential | Source | Forwarded as |\n|---|---|---|\n| GitHub | `gh auth token` | `GH_TOKEN` (+ HTTPS git credential helper) |\n| Claude/agent | `extract_claude_token` — `~/.claude/.credentials.json`, falling back to the macOS keychain blob `Claude Code-credentials` (`.claudeAiOauth.accessToken`) when the file is absent | `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` |\n| PM/Linear | lfdb credential store (`get_provider_token(\"linear\")`) | `LF_FORWARDED_PM_TOKEN` + `LF_FORWARDED_PM_PROVIDER` |\n| Doppler-backed secrets | `--secret NAME` → `doppler secrets get NAME --plain` **locally** | `export NAME=<value>` |\n\nThe forwarding preamble is piped into `ssh <host> bash -s` over stdin; `cd \"$HOME/<repo>\"`; `exec <cmd>`. Every value is single-quote escaped — secrets never touch argv, ps, or logs. Nothing is written to the remote disk.\n\n## PM env override (the new capability)\n\nPM auth is lfdb-resident, not env, so raw ssh could never forward it. `resolve_pm_token` in `ops/pm.rs` now checks `LF_FORWARDED_PM_TOKEN` **before** the store (honoring `LF_FORWARDED_PM_PROVIDER` when set). Forwarded token wins; the remote's empty store is never consulted.\n\n## Security posture (deliberately diverges from the ssh-lf.sh prototype)\n\n- **No `ssh -A` by default.** Agent forwarding hands the user's whole SSH identity to remote root. Git pushes ride the forwarded `GH_TOKEN` over HTTPS, so `-A` is dead weight. Opt-in `--forward-agent` preserves an escape hatch.\n- **Ambient git credential caching neutralized.** The forwarded `GIT_CONFIG` resets `credential.helper` to empty *before* installing the in-memory token helper, so a global store/cache/osxkeychain helper on the remote can't fire and persist the token to disk.\n- **Doppler token never forwarded.** The Doppler login/CLI token is a master key to the whole secret estate. `lf ssh` forwards specific resolved secrets, never the token that could fetch them all — resolve locally with `--secret`, forward only the value.\n\n## Tests\n\n- `build_preamble` assembles the exports + `cd` + `exec` from a stubbed bundle; asserts the git-helper reset, the forwarded secret by value, that absent credentials are omitted, and that a secret with shell metacharacters can't escape its single-quoted assignment.\n- `resolve_pm_token` returns the forwarded env token without opening the store; provider match/mismatch/blank handled.\n- env-name validation rejects injection via `--secret`.\n\nNo live ssh or API calls in tests.\n\n## Documented follow-ups (need provisioning decisions — NOT in this PR)\n\nThe security review flagged two residual credential-provisioning risks, out of scope here but captured for direction:\n\n1. **Doppler:** even with `--secret`, the *local* resolution uses the Doppler CLI login token = master key to the whole secret estate. Direction: move to a scoped read-only service token, or keep resolving locally and forwarding only what's needed (this PR does the latter for the remote; the local token is still broad).\n2. **GitHub:** the forwarded `gh` token is a broad, non-expiring classic OAuth token. Direction: switch to a fine-grained, expiring PAT scoped to the loopflow repos.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 519,
      "deletions": 6,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 833,
      "title": "lf memory: add memory log + recent-facts recency injection",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf memory log        # print facts added since the last update, oldest to newest\n```\n\n```\nGET /memory/log      # {facts} — add-stream facts since the last curation\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lf memory log` to read a wave's add-stream — the replayable facts published via `lf memory add` since the last `update` compiled them into `MEMORY.md`. It reads through the live server's replay buffer when one is running, and falls back to the journal fold otherwise (reads don't need the pen, but do require a resolvable wave context — no wave is an error, not a silent drop).\n\nIt also layers those recent facts into the wave's prompt memory. `gather_wave_memory` now renders recent stream facts (newest first, for prompt recency) above the compiled `MEMORY.md` base, so a wave's prompt sees freshly-added facts before the next curation folds them in. The `log` command still prints oldest to newest.\n\nAll changes are additive to main.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `GET /memory/log` endpoint returning `{facts}`, backed by `WaveRuntime::memory_adds()`\n- New `MemoryCommand::Log` subcommand and `read_memory_log` (live server → journal fallback)\n- `gather_wave_memory` in `wave_context` layers recent adds above the compiled base; `prompt.rs` routes through it instead of reading the origin file directly\n- Docs updated: `lf.md`, wave `README.md`, and server endpoint tables\n- `ops/rebase.rs`: TODO documenting the stacked-parent rework case a memory slice hit — interim policy is stacked worktrees target main; real fix left to the systems wave",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 214,
      "deletions": 14,
      "changed_files": 9
    },
    {
      "number": 834,
      "title": "pm/linear: teamId is ID! not String! in workflow-state filters",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op pm --status done\n```\n\nThe Linear GraphQL queries that list completed and unstarted workflow states declared `$teamId` as `String!`, but Linear's schema types `team.id` filters as `ID!`. Linear rejects the type mismatch, so the queries fail and status transitions (e.g. marking an item done) never resolve their target state. This changes both query variable declarations to `ID!` to match the schema.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `LIST_COMPLETED_WORKFLOW_STATES_QUERY`: `$teamId: String!` → `$teamId: ID!`\n- `LIST_UNSTARTED_WORKFLOW_STATES_QUERY`: `$teamId: String!` → `$teamId: ID!`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 835,
      "title": "lf cron: schedule lf commands via launchd + export-memory step",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf cron add --wave memory --flow export-memory --schedule daily\nlf cron list\nlf cron remove --wave memory --flow export-memory\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nAdds `lf cron`, a top-level command that installs local launchd jobs to run `lf` commands on a schedule, and ships the first flow built to run on it: the `export-memory` step, which compiles a wave's durable `MEMORY.md` from its base and add-stream. `lf cron` is deliberately top-level rather than under `lf op` — it's a local scheduler, not a git operation. Together these give a serverless, scheduled path for keeping a wave's memory checkpoint fresh without a live wave server.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `ops/cron` module: renders launchd plists (`loopflow.cron.<wave>.<flow>.plist`), loads/unloads via launchctl behind a `Launchctl` trait, and supports add/list/remove. v0 schedules are `daily` (runs at 03:00); jobs run `lf <flow> --wave <wave>` from the current repo with stdout/stderr to `.lf/logs`.\n- New `Cron` subcommand (`add`/`list`/`remove`) wired into `lf` with add-replaces-existing semantics.\n- New builtin `export-memory` step: reads `lf memory show`/`log`, compiles a reader-optimized `MEMORY.md`, prefers `lf memory update` when a wave server is live and falls back to writing `wave/<wave>/MEMORY.md` directly when serverless, then commits.\n- Docs for `lf cron` in `docs/lfop.md`; unit tests covering plist round-trip, schedule parsing, and step registration.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 459,
      "deletions": 4,
      "changed_files": 8
    },
    {
      "number": 836,
      "title": "rebase/queue: re-parent stacked children onto main when their parent lands",
      "body": "## Usage\n\nNo new command — this changes how the wave queue and `lf`'s rebase recovery handle a stacked child once its parent has merged. It kicks in automatically:\n\n```bash\n# parent PR (a.b) merges — plain or reworked; child a.b.c is queue head\n# the daemon reconciles the queue and re-parents a.b.c onto origin/main\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nWhen a stacked child's parent lands, the child owns only its own commits and must re-parent onto the default branch. The old lazy rebase replayed the parent's already-merged commits against `origin/main` and blocked the queue with a spurious `RebaseConflict`. This detects the landed parent and rebases with `--onto <default> <parent-tip>`, dropping the parent's history exactly — even for a multi-commit parent that the patch-id `squash_merge_fork_point` scan can't catch.\n\n\"Landed\" is detected content-independently, so a *reworked* parent (its merged content diverges from what the child stacked on) is handled too. The daemon passes its lfdb signal (`stack_status == Merged`) down through `rebase_onto_default`; where no daemon is present, the same logic infers landing from git alone (ancestor/squash-merge into the default branch, or a deleted `origin/P`). When a reworked parent diverges from lines the child also touched, the replay conflicts — that conflict surfaces to the caller rather than being auto-healed.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `git::is_merged_into` — covers both fast-forward ancestor and squash-merge shapes of a merge.\n- `ops::merged_parent_fork_point` — resolves the fork point (parent tip) and the lingering local parent ref to prune, given a `parent_landed` signal plus git-only fallbacks.\n- `plan_rebase`/`rebase_with_recovery` use the fork point to re-parent onto the default branch, replacing the old squash-only TODO path.\n- `QueueOps::rebase_onto_default` takes `parent_landed`; the reconcile loop derives it via `head_stack_parent_merged` (dotted stack parent whose inferred status is `Merged`) and prunes the merged parent's local ref after re-parenting.\n- Tests: squash-merged parent commits get dropped and the stale local ref pruned; a reworked parent overlapping the child's own lines surfaces a conflict.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 454,
      "deletions": 31,
      "changed_files": 6
    },
    {
      "number": 837,
      "title": "export-memory: give MEMORY.md compile a typed-block vocabulary",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf export-memory --wave <wave>   # on a wave with some `lf memory log` history\n```\n\n`MEMORY.md` comes back organized into `##` typed sections the agent chose — Decisions, Constraints, Glossary, How To where they fit, adapted where they don't.\n\n## Summary\n\nTeaches the `export-memory` compile to organize `MEMORY.md` into typed blocks — as a convention, not code. No parser, no `Block` type, no enforced schema. The compile prompt suggests a starting vocabulary (Decisions / Constraints / Glossary / How To) and hands the agent ownership of the shape: add a block the wave needs, drop one it doesn't, rename to fit. Structure stays predictable enough for a reader without dictating it — the same \"the fold lives in the mind\" principle, seeding how the mind thinks rather than parsing its output.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `export-memory.md` step 3 now carries the block guidance plus the explicit \"these are a starting vocabulary, not a schema — you own this file's shape\" agency line.\n- `builtins.rs` test asserts the compiled prompt mentions the block vocabulary and the agency framing.\n- No new code paths, no parser — prompt-only, per the slice design under `scratch/`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 14,
      "deletions": 0,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 838,
      "title": "wave server: collapse the five live SSE streams into one helper",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\ncargo build -p loopflow\n```\n\nThe `/events` handler in the wave server built each live SSE stream by hand-writing the same `BroadcastStream::new(rx).filter_map(...)` closure — one copy per stream (turns, ops, states, memory adds, memory summaries, inbox), each with identical lag-handling boilerplate. This collapses all of them into a single `live_stream` helper that takes a broadcast receiver and a per-value event-builder, so each call site is now one line.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `live_stream<T, F>` helper: wraps a `broadcast::Receiver` into an SSE `Event` stream, dropping lagged receivers silently (each stream resyncs on reconnect).\n- Rewrote the six per-stream closures as `live_stream` calls; the per-stream lag rationale moves to a short comment at each call site.\n- Net −15 lines, no behavior change.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 33,
      "deletions": 48,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 839,
      "title": "release: retire the central DECISIONS.md ledger",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op release run <version>\n```\n\nRelease notes are now synthesized from the merged PRs and their descriptions instead of a central `DECISIONS.md` ledger. Per-decision context lives in each wave's `MEMORY.md`, so the release pipeline no longer reads, injects, or archives a decisions ledger. The `release/unreleased/` → `release/v<version>/` promotion still happens for any staged artifacts.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Drop `load_unreleased_decisions` and the `decisions` field on `ReleaseNotesContext`; `generate_release_notes` no longer takes a decisions argument or emits a `## Release decisions` section.\n- Update the `release-notes` and `release` builtin prompts/steps to draw intent from merged PRs rather than pasting `DECISIONS.md`, pointing per-decision context at each wave's `MEMORY.md`.\n- Rework tests: promotion tests use a generic `CHANGES.md` staged artifact, and the fallback test asserts notes come from PRs (no `## Release decisions`) while the unreleased dir is still promoted.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 21,
      "deletions": 69,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 840,
      "title": "journal: lock events.jsonl appends + add lf op reset-waves",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op reset-waves        # confirm, then kill lf-* tmux sessions + clear stale endpoints\nlf op reset-waves -y     # skip the prompt\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo fixes for wave operations. Concurrent journal writers were corrupting `events.jsonl` — separate processes appending unlocked could interleave a partial line into another's, leaving unparseable JSONL. Appends now take an exclusive `flock` (Unix) and write each event as a single buffered `write_all`, so lines stay whole under contention. A multi-process regression test spawns 8 child writers emitting 20 large events each and asserts every line parses.\n\nThe new `lf op reset-waves` gives operators a fresh-start button: it kills every `lf-*` tmux session (wave servers and workers, which SIGHUP their process groups) and removes now-stale `wave/<name>/.wave-endpoint` pointers under the main repo so nothing dangles. It prompts before killing unless `-y` is passed or stdin isn't a terminal.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `journal`: `flock`-guarded, single-write appends to `events.jsonl`; no-op lock on non-Unix; cross-process concurrency regression test\n- `lf op reset-waves` command with `-y/--yes`, wired into the ops CLI and excluded from `ops` item flows",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 228,
      "deletions": 6,
      "changed_files": 4
    },
    {
      "number": 843,
      "title": "waves: make GOAL.md the durable charter",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\nlf wave goals\nlf build \"probe the next goal-authored reference build\" --wave goals --dispatch\n```\n\nOpen any `wave/*/GOAL.md`: the charter now leads with Objective, Measures, Cron, and Process. Routing judgment lives in Process; `primary_flow` is gone from the wave DTO/storage shape.\n\nValidation run locally:\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy -- -D warnings\ncargo test --all\nuv run pytest python/tests/\ncargo nextest run --all\ncd website && uv run python dev.py test\nswift test --package-path swift -Xswiftc -gnone\ntests/e2e/test_smoke.sh\n```\n\nThe Concerto UI xcodebuild target built and passed its 301 unit tests, but the UI screenshot target hung locally in XCTest/LaunchServices before the test body ran (`waiting for workers to materialize`). Recheck that target in CI or a clean macOS UI-test environment.\nFresh gate rerun: default DerivedData hit a local linker write error for `ConcertoUITests`; retrying with an isolated `-derivedDataPath` reached `Testing started` and then hung waiting for UI runner workers to materialize / `-runningDidFinish`. The package-level Swift tests above are green.\n\n## Intent\n\nMake GOAL.md the wave's durable charter instead of a mixed bag of default flow, metrics, and prompt prose. A wave now names what it exists to do, how it measures progress, what recurring duties it owns, and how it chooses work. The code mirrors that shape by removing `primary_flow` from the wave model and keeping concrete flow names on runs/dispatches where work actually executes.\n\n## Assumptions\n\nLinear remains the live roadmap. MEMORY.md remains server-owned. Cron frontmatter stays structured for the current parser. A second objective belongs in a child wave, not in another GOAL.md section.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Added a forward migration for `primary_flow` removal instead of editing historical migrations.\n- Kept the code migration minimal; the main product value is the substantive wave-charter rewrite.\n- Updated Rust, Python, Swift, and fixture DTOs together so absent `primary_flow` is the only wire shape.\n- Removed stale user-facing examples that taught `primary_flow` frontmatter.\n- Kept `wave/goals/MEMORY.md` out of the PR diff; durable memory updates belong through `lf memory`.\n\n## Not included\n\nNo GOAL.md elicitation UX, no phase-B onboarding work, no manual edit of server-owned `MEMORY.md`, and no broader cleanup of legacy flow parsing.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1057,
      "deletions": 402,
      "changed_files": 66
    },
    {
      "number": 845,
      "title": "step → skill, mind → flowloop: one generic looping driver",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf skill implement          # run a single skill\nlf flow build               # run a flow\nlf wave designer            # a wave is a looping flow (flowloop)\nlf commit                   # gate + commit\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nTwo renames and one consolidation. The prompt unit is now a **skill** everywhere (formerly \"step\"): `.lf/steps/` → `.lf/skills/`, built-in `steps/` → `skills/`, the `Step`/`ConcreteStep`/`FlowStep` types → `Skill`/`ConcreteSkill`/`FlowSkill`, `--skill` flag, and every DTO field (`step_index` → `skill_index`, `step` → `skill`) across Rust, Python, and Swift, backed by migration `054_step_to_skill.sql`. The persistent wave \"mind\" is renamed to **flowloop** and rebuilt as a single generic driver: a flowloop is just a looping flow where the agent sets the termination bit and the runner checks it. The old tiered mind (`wave/mind.rs`, ~2.5k lines) and the legacy persistent-thread scheduler are deleted in favor of `flowloop/` (`driver`, `pass`, `run`, `wave`) with a shared `FlowloopRun` lifecycle. Flows now go bare — task/project/wave are reached via explicit `lf flow`/`lf skill` verbs rather than tier-specific entry points.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `rust/loopflow/src/flowloop/` module: one runtime, generic looping driver; `MindState` → `FlowloopState`, `/health mind` → `flowloop`\n- `lf commit` command added (gate + implement)\n- Deleted the workstyle converter (`python/loopflow/workstyle/convert.py`, tests, `refresh-gstack` step, `workstyle.yaml`)\n- New per-tier build skills: `{task,project,wave}_{clarify,mutate,pursue}.md` and `flow/{task,project,wave}.yaml`\n- Swift `Step.swift` → `Skill.swift`; DTO fixtures and goldens updated to the new wire shape\n- Docs, README, website, and e2e smoke follow the rename",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 4933,
      "deletions": 6599,
      "changed_files": 288
    },
    {
      "number": 846,
      "title": "concerto: show objective and project plan in the wave viewer",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nswift test --package-path swift -Xswiftc -gnone --filter WavePlanParserTests\nswift test --package-path swift -Xswiftc -gnone --filter PortfolioRepoStateTests\nuv run python scripts/test.py\n```\n\nLaunch Concerto against this repo and select the `concerto` wave. The detail pane now splits: the local wave plan on the left — the Objective from `wave/concerto/GOAL.md` and the live projects from `wave/concerto/projects/*.md` — beside the existing WaveChat surface. In the repo wave list, rows sort by attention priority so `failed` sits above `waiting`, `running`, then idle.\n\n## Summary\n\nMoves Concerto's wave viewer onto the new flowloop shape without adding a backend path. The wave charter now carries only the Objective; the measures live in one markdown file per project under `projects/`, and the app renders that local plan next to the vendor-owned WaveChat conversation. Slice 1 is deliberately local-file only — a `WavePlanParser` reads the wave directory directly rather than introducing new lfd wire shape or an `lf wave show` query. Alongside the viewer, this branch retires the dead `LocalWaveService` (~1500 lines) and its auth tests, consolidating durable reads onto the daemonless `lf`-over-`lfdb` `RegistryQuery` path and folding an `lf`-shaped `RunStatus` model into `Run`. A small `ResolvedLfCache` memoizes `lf` binary resolution to avoid repeated executable probes.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `WavePlan` / `WaveProject` frontend domain state (not wire DTOs) plus `WavePlanParser`, attached to `WaveViewModel` for both live registry rows and authored-on-disk placeholders.\n- `WaveDetailPane` splits into an `HSplitView` of plan + WaveChat when a local plan exists; falls back to WaveChat-only otherwise (remote/iOS, waves without authored files).\n- Repo wave list sorts by attention priority (`failed` → `waiting` → `running` → idle) so blocking work isn't buried under healthy sessions.\n- Deleted `LocalWaveService.swift`, `WaveServiceProtocol.swift`, and `LocalWaveServiceAuthTests`; added a leaner `WaveService`. `Run` gains an `lf`-biased `RunStatus` (lowercase tokens, loud on unknown, no invented `cancelled`).\n- Restarted `wave/concerto/GOAL.md` around the Objective and split the KRs into five live `projects/*.md` files; folded branch design/decisions into `wave/concerto/MEMORY.md`.\n\n## Not included\n\n- Runs ledger, charting, pubsub live-run updates, and attachable session rows.\n- Linear task ingestion or per-project task grouping (Linear task tier remains flat).\n- New lfd endpoints, DTO fixture changes, or remote plan loading.\n\nGate validation passed the full changed-aware suite (Rust fmt/clippy/test, `pytest`, website, Swift package tests, e2e smoke). The CI-equivalent Concerto `xcodebuild` run built the app and passed 5 XCTest + 304 Swift Testing tests after one `xcodebuild clean` for stale DerivedData; `ConcertoUITests-Runner` still exited early before bootstrapping XCTest in this headless environment.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1849,
      "deletions": 2365,
      "changed_files": 37
    },
    {
      "number": 848,
      "title": "rename: Concerto → Loopflow across swift, tooling, docs, and website",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\ncd swift && xcodegen generate\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -project LoopflowSwift.xcodeproj -scheme LoopflowMac -destination platform=macOS CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=NO\n\nuv run python scripts/loopflow-dev.py run-debug   # build + launch lfd + Loopflow (macOS)\nuv run python scripts/test.py --loopflow          # force the Loopflow UI suite\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch renames the desktop/mobile product from **Concerto** to **Loopflow**, aligning the app with the framework name. The change is mechanical but cross-cutting: it restructures the Swift package (`LoopflowCore` + `Concerto` app → a shared `Loopflow` library plus `LoopflowMac`/`LoopflowiOS` app targets), renames every tooling/CI/deploy reference, and sweeps the docs and website. Along the way it fixes fallout from the restructure — pinning `PRODUCT_MODULE_NAME` so the app module no longer shadows the `Loopflow` framework, and relocating over-pulled files to their correct platform target (library holds core + `AppBootstrap`).\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Swift restructure**: `swift/Concerto` → `swift/LoopflowMac`, `swift/LoopflowCore` → `swift/Loopflow` (shared lib), new `swift/LoopflowiOS`; `Package.swift`/`project.yml` retargeted; platform-specific files moved out of the shared library into `LoopflowMac`. New `AppBootstrap.swift` and per-platform `LoopflowApp.swift` entry points.\n- **Config/keys**: `ConcertoConfig` → `LoopflowConfig`; remote-connection UserDefaults domain `com.loopflow.concerto` → `com.loopflow.mac`.\n- **Rust**: `Surface` enum variants `ConcertoMac`/`ConcertoIphone` → `Mac`/`Iphone`; prompt and command references updated.\n- **CI/tooling**: `concerto-ui-test` job → `loopflow-ui-test`; `scheme Concerto` → `LoopflowMac`; scripts `concerto-dev.py` → `loopflow-dev.py`, `release-concerto.py` → `release-loopflow.py`; `test.py --concerto` → `--loopflow`.\n- **Website**: Concerto → Loopflow throughout, Symphonia teams page removed.\n- **Docs**: README, TESTING, STYLE, PROMPT_STYLE, deploy guides, and `.lf` flow/step prompts reworded.\n- Design note on the rename under `scratch/concerto-to-loopflow-rename.md`.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 861,
      "deletions": 887,
      "changed_files": 235
    },
    {
      "number": 850,
      "title": "install.py: drop stale --global from lf op sync-skills",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\npython scripts/install.py\n```\n\n## Summary\n\n`lf op sync-skills` no longer accepts a `--global` flag, so the installer's skill-sync step was passing a dead argument. This drops `--global` from the `_sync_skills` invocation, leaving just `op sync-skills --yes`, and updates the corresponding test to match.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `scripts/install.py`: `_sync_skills` now runs `lf op sync-skills --yes`\n- `python/tests/test_install_script.py`: renamed and updated the sync-skills test to assert the new argument list",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3,
      "deletions": 3,
      "changed_files": 2
    },
    {
      "number": 851,
      "title": "agents: respect sandbox configs in worktrees",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```yaml\n# loop.yaml — normal floor, no config needed; Loopflow supplies conservative defaults\n# Codex: workspace-write + approval never (non-interactive); Claude: skip prompts (non-interactive)\n\nyolo: true   # explicit bypass: Codex --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox, Claude --dangerously-skip-permissions\n```\n\nRun any agent from a Git worktree and it just works — staging, commits, rebases, and `lf op` can now write the linked worktree index under the main repo.\n\n## Summary\n\nLoopflow used to hardcode Codex/Claude permission flags, which both clobbered a user's more-permissive vendor config and broke agents running inside Git worktrees (Git needs to write `<main>/.git/worktrees/<worktree>/` but that path lives outside the worktree cwd). This PR makes Loopflow treat its permissions as a *floor*: it reads the effective Codex `config.toml` and Claude `settings.json` for the launch directory, supplies its conservative default only when the vendor config is unset or less permissive, leaves already-permissive configs (e.g. `danger-full-access`, `bypassPermissions`) untouched, and warns when it has to raise a weaker config. Claude and Codex CLI/TUI sessions launched from a worktree automatically add the main repo as an extra writable directory so Git worktree metadata stays writable without loosening the sandbox.\n\n## Changes\n\n- Parse Codex `sandbox_mode`/`approval_policy` (home + repo `.codex/config.toml`) and Claude `permissions.defaultMode` (`~/.claude` + repo settings), ordered by permissiveness so Loopflow's floor only raises, never downgrades\n- `codex_permission_args` / `claude_skip_permissions` replace the old hardcoded flag logic; both warn when raising a weaker vendor config to the Loopflow floor\n- `yolo: true` now maps Codex to `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox` (was `--sandbox danger-full-access`)\n- `workspace_add_dirs` adds the main repo via `--add-dir` (Claude/Codex) when cwd is a worktree; `ClaudeArgs` gains an `add_dirs` field\n- docs/config.md rewrites the Yolo section and adds a Worktree Sandboxes section\n- Tests cover config parsing, floor-vs-downgrade behavior, yolo bypass, and a real Git worktree fixture",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 708,
      "deletions": 53,
      "changed_files": 5
    },
    {
      "number": 852,
      "title": "lf op pm: attach Linear tasks to wave projects",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\nlf op pm status\nlf op pm show --wave product\nlf op pm show --wave product --project wave-chat\nlf op pm task create --wave product --project wave-chat --title \"Retain steward thread after restart\"\nlf op pm task move --id <task-id> --wave product --project loopflow-api\nlf op pm rename --wave product --title \"Product\"\nlf op pm sync --plan\n```\n\nValidation run:\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --check\ncargo clippy -- -D warnings\ncargo test -p loopflow pm\ncargo test -p loopflow golden_prompt\nuv run python scripts/test.py\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all\n```\n\n`scripts/test.py` passed the changed-aware Rust and website suites. `scripts/test.py --all` passed Python, Rust, website, Swift package, and e2e smoke. The Loopflow UI suite is blocked locally: the first full run failed in shared DerivedData while linking `LoopflowUITests`; a fresh-DerivedData retry built and ran the app/unit bundle, reported 304 passing tests, then `LoopflowUITests-Runner` exited before establishing the UI-test connection and Xcode returned 65.\n\n## Intent\n\nMake Loopflow's planning model explicit in code and docs: waves are durable operating contexts, projects are measured bets under one wave, and tasks live in Linear. `lf op pm` can now group, filter, create, move, close, diagnose, and rename Linear tasks/projects in terms of the local wave project tree.\n\nGate polish aligned the core built-in prompts and architecture docs with that model so agents now see the canonical `lf op pm task ...` surface instead of the compatibility `pm update` path.\n\n## Assumptions\n\nEach wave has at most one backing Linear project in `wave/<wave>/GOAL.md`. Local projects are markdown files under `wave/<wave>/projects/`. Linear labels named `project:<slug>` are the visible association between tasks and local projects.\n\nLive Linear mutation demos were not run in gate because they would create labels, move issues, close tasks, or rename real Linear projects.\n\n## Key Decisions\n\nOne Linear project remains the task container for each wave; local Loopflow projects are represented as labels instead of additional Linear projects. `lf op pm update` remains compatible, but the documented and prompt-visible API is now `lf op pm task ...`. `sync --plan` reports ambiguous drift instead of guessing task migrations.\n\n## Not Included\n\nNo automatic migration of ambiguous existing Linear tasks. No stale-label removal during task moves. No PM provider beyond the current Linear path. No Swift/UI behavior changes.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2245,
      "deletions": 3383,
      "changed_files": 120
    },
    {
      "number": 853,
      "title": "lf: promote ops into top-level commands",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\n```bash\nlf pr                  # show current branch PR state\nlf pr open             # create/update the current branch PR\nlf pr submit           # prep for a human merge\nlf pr land             # hands-off merge path\nlf wt list\nlf wt create next-thing\nlf commit -m \"message\"\nlf release run patch\nlf pm show --wave systems\nlf pm task create --wave systems --project ops --title \"Tighten the gate\"\n```\n\nFlow operation items use the same command payloads:\n\n```yaml\n- gate\n- op: pr land --create-pr\n```\n\nValidation:\n\n```bash\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\ncargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test\nswift test --package-path swift -Xswiftc -gnone\nuv run pytest python/tests/\nuv run python scripts/test.py --all\n```\n\nCurrent local result: Python, Rust, website, Swift package, and e2e passed. The\nLoopflow UI Xcode job builds and passes app/unit tests here, but the\n`LoopflowUITests-Runner` exits before bootstrapping in this headless session.\n\n## Intent\n\nRemove the `lf op` drawer from the human command API and make common mechanical\ncommands first-class. Humans, builtin flows, webhook execs, docs, prompts,\nSwift-launched session commands, and smoke tests now use one command grammar.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n`op:` remains the flow-step marker; only its payload changes to the promoted CLI\ngrammar. `sync-skills` remains hidden instead of documented because the install\npath still needs a direct non-interactive sync command. Historical release\nnotes and recorded fixtures keep old `lf op` text because they are shipped\nrecords or captured data.\n\n## Key decisions\n\nBare `lf pr` reports status, while `lf pr open`, `lf pr submit`, and\n`lf pr land` carry mutating lifecycle actions. Main's newer Linear task/project\nsurface was preserved under `lf pm` during the rebase. The retired human CLI\nverbs (`next`, `branches`, `doctor`, `shell`, `cp`, `push`, `sync`, and queue\nreconcile) are gone from clap; machine callers use library paths or validated\nnew argv.\n\n## Not included\n\nNo `lf op` compatibility shim, no old flow-payload migration, and no edits to\nhistorical release artifacts. The branch was rebased locally and not pushed.\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1302,
      "deletions": 2266,
      "changed_files": 123
    },
    {
      "number": 854,
      "title": "release: make weekly PR copy deterministic",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf op release\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe weekly release PR was previously handed to `land` with `pr_title: None` / `pr_body: None`, which meant the title and body were generated by an agent at land time — nondeterministic copy for a PR whose contents are already fully known. Release PRs now carry copy derived straight from the release itself: the title reuses the same `release: v<version>` string as the release commit message, and the body is the generated `RELEASE_NOTES.md` already sitting in the worktree.\n\nSame source of truth, no LLM in the loop, and the PR body matches the notes that ship with the tag.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `release_pr_copy` builds a `PrCopy` from the release commit message and `RELEASE_NOTES.md`\n- `prepare_release_in_worktree` passes that copy through to `land` instead of leaving title/body unset\n- Test covers the title format and that the body is the generated notes verbatim",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 23,
      "deletions": 2,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 856,
      "title": "minds: lf serve, lf loop, and the agent bus",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf serve designer                                  # boot the mind: listener, thread, playhead\nlf chat --steer \"ship the button audit first\"      # steer the body now playing, else queue\nlf loop task \"fix the flaky timeout test\" -w designer          # inhabit a bounded loop\nlf loop build \"audit the settings panes\" -w designer --detach  # delegate one\n\nlf radio \"landed PR #91, tests green\"        # broadcast on your channel\nlf radio -c infra.148e \"rebase and retry\"    # steer one hand\nlf sub infra                                 # hear the wave and its family\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nA wave is a mind you can talk to; everything else is a bounded loop it hands off. This PR makes that split real in the command surface and in the runtime beneath it.\n\n`lf wave <name>` becomes `lf serve <name>`: it boots a listener, a durable thread, and a **playhead** that gives each flow step its own live harness session. `lf task` and `lf <flow> --dispatch` collapse into `lf loop <flow> \"<text>\"`, which repeats a flow in a child worktree until the flow's skills write `done` to `scratch/loop.yaml`. Without `--detach` it blocks; with it, the live wave server owns the run and hands back a read-only tmux session.\n\nMachine speech gets its own wire. `lf radio` is an INSERT into the shared store — no server in the path, so a broadcast survives a sleeping wave and folds into its thread, attributed, on the next sweep. `lf sub` tunes in at the head of a channel prefix (`infra` hears `infra.148e`). Channels form a dot tree, which retires per-child journals: a hand's byline comes from the channel it spoke on, not from a journal of its own. `lf chat` stays the human surface — durable, replayed, owned by a served mind — and gains `--steer` to reach the body mid-turn.\n\nThe back half of the branch is compression against that new shape. Child journals, the four-field open turn, the loop file's two keys, duplicate playhead step identity, and a hand-copied test wiring all collapse to one representation each. `lf chat`, `wave/runtime.rs`, `wave/server.rs`, and `lfd/executor/helpers.rs` shrink accordingly.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `lf serve` replaces `lf wave`; `lf loop <flow>` replaces `lf task` and `--dispatch`\n- New `wave/bus.rs` + migration `059_bus.sql`: the store is the bus, readers sweep it, gaps outlive the rows\n- New `wave/playhead.rs` and `lf playhead`: one live harness session per flow step\n- `lf thread` splits out of `lf sub`; `lf radio` is the agent-facing broadcast verb\n- `project-promote` flow and skill, backed by a new `ops/project.rs`\n- Wire types updated across Rust, Swift, and the DTO fixtures: bylines are strings, channel-tagged turns are gone, `wave_flowloop_states` → `wave_loop_states`\n- Docs, README, LOOPFLOW.md, and the demo scripts moved off the retired verbs and off \"flowloop\" as a noun\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 8105,
      "deletions": 4357,
      "changed_files": 134
    },
    {
      "number": 857,
      "title": "Make the local run ledger trustworthy and visible",
      "body": "## Try it!\n\nBuild this branch's CLI, then inspect the same evidence used by the dashboard:\n\n```bash\ncargo build -p loopflow --bin lf\nexport PATH=\"$PWD/target/debug:$PATH\"\n\nlf doctor\nlf runs\nlf usage\nlf usage --json --days 30\nlf tokens\nlf tokens --days 365\n```\n\nOn the real local ledger, all six doctor checks are green and eight usage\nboundaries reconcile exactly: 20,010 JSON tokens equal the 20,010-token human\nsummary. At this revision, the live tree and the final historical snapshot also\nagree exactly at 183,599 lines and 1,791,384 tokens; the cached 92-snapshot year\nloads in 2.55 seconds.\n\nExercise the Mac dashboard:\n\n```bash\nuv run python scripts/loopflow-dev.py run-debug\n```\n\nOpen **Go → Telemetry (⌘1)**. The page shows ledger health, tokens by skill and\nmodel, cache reuse, codebase growth, and a zoomable code-weight icicle.\n\nExercise wave chat in two terminals:\n\n```bash\nlf wave intelligence --no-flowloop\nlf wavechat intelligence\n```\n\nThe second pane replays recent history, follows live events, accepts messages,\nand provides `/status`, `/help`, and `/quit`.\n\n## Intent\n\nMake local agent work observable from one trustworthy record. Canonical process\nidentity and cumulative usage boundaries now support audits, trace trees, spend\nreconciliation, codebase-weight history, and a native dashboard without a\nparallel telemetry service or a second interpretation of the data.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Repository journals remain available if old events ever need a deliberate\n  import after migration 057 clears the ambiguous pre-contract ledger.\n- Provider usage snapshots are cumulative within a process; `own_spend` turns\n  adjacent boundaries into attributable increments.\n- Git's tracked blob is the source of truth for historical code weight,\n  including symlink link text rather than the target's contents.\n- The Mac app bundles the `lf` built from the same revision. The development\n  installer establishes that invariant.\n- Local SQLite and Git history are sufficient for this wave; remote telemetry\n  is outside the current bet.\n\n## Key decisions\n\n- Treat each run as a trace and each launched process as a span, with explicit\n  parent identity.\n- Use the same boundary rows for JSON and human usage summaries, including\n  flows that launch multiple providers or models.\n- Read telemetry directly in Rust and keep Swift as a JSON CLI client instead\n  of maintaining an lfd HTTP mirror.\n- Cache historical token counts by blob SHA and make the live scanner match Git\n  semantics for tracked symlinks.\n- Align the local Loopflow UI check with CI's `build-for-testing` merge gate.\n\n## Not included\n\nThe eval harvester, PR delivery records, `escalated` emission, wavechat\npause/resume/interrupt verbs, remote telemetry, and automatic backfill of\npre-contract events remain future work.\n\n",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 3938,
      "deletions": 1196,
      "changed_files": 50
    },
    {
      "number": 858,
      "title": "build(deps): bump actions/cache from 4 to 6",
      "body": "Bumps [actions/cache](https://github.com/actions/cache) from 4 to 6.\n<details>\n<summary>Release notes</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/releases\">actions/cache's releases</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h2>v6.0.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update packages, migrate to ESM by <a href=\"https://github.com/Samirat\"><code>@​Samirat</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1760\">actions/cache#1760</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v6.0.0\">https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v6.0.0</a></p>\n<h2>v5.1.0</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump <code>@​actions/cache</code> to v5.1.0 - handle read-only cache access by <a href=\"https://github.com/jasongin\"><code>@​jasongin</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1775\">actions/cache#1775</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.1.0\">https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.1.0</a></p>\n<h2>v5.0.5</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Update ts-http-runtime dependency by <a href=\"https://github.com/yacaovsnc\"><code>@​yacaovsnc</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1747\">actions/cache#1747</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.5\">https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.5</a></p>\n<h2>v5.0.4</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Add release instructions and update maintainer docs by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1696\">actions/cache#1696</a></li>\n<li>Potential fix for code scanning alert no. 52: Workflow does not contain permissions by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1697\">actions/cache#1697</a></li>\n<li>Fix workflow permissions and cleanup workflow names / formatting by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1699\">actions/cache#1699</a></li>\n<li>docs: Update examples to use the latest version by <a href=\"https://github.com/XZTDean\"><code>@​XZTDean</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1690\">actions/cache#1690</a></li>\n<li>Fix proxy integration tests by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1701\">actions/cache#1701</a></li>\n<li>Fix cache key in examples.md for bun.lock by <a href=\"https://github.com/RyPeck\"><code>@​RyPeck</code></a> in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1722\">actions/cache#1722</a></li>\n<li>Update dependencies &amp; patch security vulnerabilities by <a href=\"https://github.com/Link\"><code>@​Link</code></a>- in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1738\">actions/cache#1738</a></li>\n</ul>\n<h2>New Contributors</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/XZTDean\"><code>@​XZTDean</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1690\">actions/cache#1690</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/RyPeck\"><code>@​RyPeck</code></a> made their first contribution in <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/pull/1722\">actions/cache#1722</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.4\">https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.4</a></p>\n<h2>v5.0.3</h2>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/cache</code> to v5.0.5 (Resolves: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/security/dependabot/33\">https://github.com/actions/cache/security/dependabot/33</a>)</li>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/core</code> to v2.0.3</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Full Changelog</strong>: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.3\">https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v5...v5.0.3</a></p>\n<h2>v.5.0.2</h2>\n<h1>v5.0.2</h1>\n<h2>What's Changed</h2>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Changelog</summary>\n<p><em>Sourced from <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/RELEASES.md\">actions/cache's changelog</a>.</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<h1>Releases</h1>\n<h2>How to prepare a release</h2>\n<blockquote>\n<p>[!NOTE]\nRelevant for maintainers with write access only.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li>Switch to a new branch from <code>main</code>.</li>\n<li>Run <code>npm test</code> to ensure all tests are passing.</li>\n<li>Update the version in <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/package.json\"><code>https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/package.json</code></a>.</li>\n<li>Run <code>npm run build</code> to update the compiled files.</li>\n<li>Update this <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/RELEASES.md\"><code>https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/RELEASES.md</code></a> with the new version and changes in the <code>## Changelog</code> section.</li>\n<li>Run <code>licensed cache</code> to update the license report.</li>\n<li>Run <code>licensed status</code> and resolve any warnings by updating the <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/.licensed.yml\"><code>https://github.com/actions/cache/blob/main/.licensed.yml</code></a> file with the exceptions.</li>\n<li>Commit your changes and push your branch upstream.</li>\n<li>Open a pull request against <code>main</code> and get it reviewed and merged.</li>\n<li>Draft a new release <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/releases\">https://github.com/actions/cache/releases</a> use the same version number used in <code>package.json</code>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a new tag with the version number.</li>\n<li>Auto generate release notes and update them to match the changes you made in <code>RELEASES.md</code>.</li>\n<li>Toggle the set as the latest release option.</li>\n<li>Publish the release.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n<li>Navigate to <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/actions/workflows/release-new-action-version.yml\">https://github.com/actions/cache/actions/workflows/release-new-action-version.yml</a>\n<ol>\n<li>There should be a workflow run queued with the same version number.</li>\n<li>Approve the run to publish the new version and update the major tags for this action.</li>\n</ol>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Changelog</h2>\n<h3>6.1.0</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/cache</code> to v6.1.0 to pick up <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/toolkit/pull/2435\">actions/toolkit#2435 Handle cache write error due to read-only token</a></li>\n<li>Switch redundant &quot;Cache save failed&quot; warning to debug log in save-only</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>6.0.0</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Updated <code>@actions/cache</code> to ^6.0.1, <code>@actions/core</code> to ^3.0.1, <code>@actions/exec</code> to ^3.0.0, <code>@actions/io</code> to ^3.0.2</li>\n<li>Migrated to ESM module system</li>\n<li>Upgraded Jest to v30 and test infrastructure to be ESM compatible</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5.0.4</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump <code>minimatch</code> to v3.1.5 (fixes ReDoS via globstar patterns)</li>\n<li>Bump <code>undici</code> to v6.24.1 (WebSocket decompression bomb protection, header validation fixes)</li>\n<li>Bump <code>fast-xml-parser</code> to v5.5.6</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5.0.3</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/cache</code> to v5.0.5 (Resolves: <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/security/dependabot/33\">https://github.com/actions/cache/security/dependabot/33</a>)</li>\n<li>Bump <code>@actions/core</code> to v2.0.3</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>5.0.2</h3>\n<!-- raw HTML omitted -->\n</blockquote>\n<p>... (truncated)</p>\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>Commits</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/55cc8345863c7cc4c66a329aec7e433d2d1c52a9\"><code>55cc834</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/issues/1768\">#1768</a> from jasongin/readonly-cache</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/d8cd72f230726cdf4457ebb61ec1b593a8d12337\"><code>d8cd72f</code></a> Bump <code>@​actions/cache</code> to v6.1.0 - handle cache write error due to RO token</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/2c8a9bd7457de244a408f35966fab2fb45fda9c8\"><code>2c8a9bd</code></a> Merge pull request <a href=\"https://redirect.github.com/actions/cache/issues/1760\">#1760</a> from actions/samirat/esm_migration_and_package_update</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/e9b91fdc3fea7d79165fceb79042ef45c2d51023\"><code>e9b91fd</code></a> Prettier fixes</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/e4884b8ff7f92ef6b52c79eda480bbc86e685adb\"><code>e4884b8</code></a> Rebuild dist</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/10baf0191a3c426ea0fa4a3253a5c04233b6e18f\"><code>10baf01</code></a> Fixed licenses</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/e39b386c9004d72a15d864ade8c0b3a702d47a37\"><code>e39b386</code></a> Fix test mock return order</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/b6928203372a8571ff984c0c883ef3a1adfb0c06\"><code>b692820</code></a> PR feedback</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/60749128a44d25d3c520a489e576380cf00ff3f1\"><code>6074912</code></a> Rebuild dist bundles as ESM to match type:module</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/commit/5a912e8b4af820fa082a0e75cfd2c782f8fbfe0e\"><code>5a912e8</code></a> Fix lint and jest issues</li>\n<li>Additional commits viewable in <a href=\"https://github.com/actions/cache/compare/v4...v6\">compare view</a></li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n<br />\n\n\n[![Dependabot compatibility score](https://dependabot-badges.githubapp.com/badges/compatibility_score?dependency-name=actions/cache&package-manager=github_actions&previous-version=4&new-version=6)](https://docs.github.com/en/github/managing-security-vulnerabilities/about-dependabot-security-updates#about-compatibility-scores)\n\nDependabot will resolve any conflicts with this PR as long as you don't alter it yourself. You can also trigger a rebase manually by commenting `@dependabot rebase`.\n\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-start)\n[//]: # (dependabot-automerge-end)\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary>Dependabot commands and options</summary>\n<br />\n\nYou can trigger Dependabot actions by commenting on this PR:\n- `@dependabot rebase` will rebase this PR\n- `@dependabot recreate` will recreate this PR, overwriting any edits that have been made to it\n- `@dependabot show <dependency name> ignore conditions` will show all of the ignore conditions of the specified dependency\n- `@dependabot ignore this major version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this major version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this minor version` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this minor version (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n- `@dependabot ignore this dependency` will close this PR and stop Dependabot creating any more for this dependency (unless you reopen the PR or upgrade to it yourself)\n\n\n</details>",
