    {
      "number": 863,
      "title": "product: make wave chat KRs measurable",
      "body": "## What changed\n\nThe Wave Chat project's KRs are rewritten as measurable proofs and the four-item \"Open questions\" section is dropped.\n\n```\nwave/product/projects/wave-chat.md | 6 insertions, 50 deletions\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThe old KRs read as aspirations (\"stays coherent,\" \"survives every boundary\") paired with a long open-questions section debating steer semantics, CLI surface overlap, and hand steer channels. This tightens the project so a maintainer can tell when the bet holds: each KR now carries an explicit window (one month of use, one week of dogfood), a pass condition (5/5 boundary trials, 10/10 cold-start reviews, N/N send/steer/interrupt targeting), and a named failure event (unsourced fact breaks the streak, any truncated wave memory fails). The open-questions block is removed — the resolved product decisions (steer degrades honestly and reports mid-turn vs. queued; `lf chat` owns the human thread, `lf sub` stays bus-only) are folded directly into the KRs as the observable end states they now assert.\n\n## Changes\n\n- KRs restated as proofs with time windows, trial counts, and explicit failure events\n- Steer semantics resolved into a KR: each steer is reported as mid-turn or queued for the next turn boundary across Codex, Claude, and OpenCode\n- CLI ownership resolved into a KR: `lf chat` sole human-thread owner, `lf sub` bus-only, zero overlapping commands\n- \"Open questions\" section deleted",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 6,
      "deletions": 50,
      "changed_files": 1
    },
    {
      "number": 864,
      "title": "execution model: inline-first delegation and cross-command flag normalization",
      "body": "## Try it\n\n```bash\nlf --wave designer loop task \"fix the flaky chord-timeout test\"   # canonical\nlf loop task \"fix the flaky test\" --wave designer                # accepted, normalized\nlf pm task --wave designer create --title \"Fix it\"               # flag crosses to the owning subcommand\nlf flow scan-pass \"scan the runtime\"                             # one pass, no loop worktree\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nReframes how waves execute work: the current process and worktree are the default surface, not a dispatch desk. The universal `LOOPFLOW.md` becomes a capability floor teaching inline execution plus mechanical `lf` operations, while wave/project/task tier skills each add only the orchestration their tier owns. A wave resolves its sole blocking move inline and spins off a child loop only for a strict subset that earns its own repeated lifecycle or real parallelism — replacing the old \"coordinate, rarely write code, delegate self-sufficient work to workers\" model. Docs, builtin skills, `LOOPFLOW.md`, and the prompt goldens are rewritten to match, and every example moves global scope ahead of the subcommand (`lf --wave <w> loop …`), the order `--detach` actually requires.\n\nTo make that canonical order ergonomic, the `lf` arg pre-parser now derives flag ownership recursively from the clap definition instead of a hand-maintained top-level list. Unambiguous global flags are accepted on either side of a built-in command and normalized onto the subcommand that owns the spelling; local collisions (`-m` on `commit`, loop-local `--max-turns`) stay put, and `--` ends normalization so literal flag-shaped text survives.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `LOOPFLOW.md` slimmed to a universal execution floor; PM discovery, server startup, and loop recipes moved into the tier skills that exercise them (`wave_pursue`, `project_pursue`, `task_pursue` — tasks never invoke `lf loop`).\n- `bin/lf.rs`: recursive `CommandArgTables`/`FlagTables` built from clap so nested subcommand flags can never drift from the definition; normalization routes a flag to the selected command path with local spellings winning on collision.\n- Loops now reject fewer than two passes before any wave/worktree/server resolution; `lf flow` is the one-shot surface (no `--max-passes 1` degenerate loop).\n- Placed loops carry one minted run id through the registry, detached HTTP handoff, pass env (`LF_RUN_ID`, clearing `LF_PROCESS_ID`), prompt logs, ledger, and a `wave.<run>` bus channel, so status, `lf trace`, and reports describe one unit of work.\n- Explicit top-level `--wave` is now an identity claim requiring a matching local registry row; it fails early rather than assembling one wave's context under another's attribution.\n- `session.rs` drops a dead `!inherited` guard in the explicit-wave path; `lf commit` gains gating; docs (`lf.md`, `waves.md`, `wave-authoring.md`, `getting-started.md`, `config.md`) and README updated to the inline-first framing.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 1657,
      "deletions": 1242,
      "changed_files": 74
    },
    {
      "number": 865,
      "title": "lf pm: map waves to Linear Initiatives and refresh OAuth automatically",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf auth linear                            # reconnect once to record the PKCE client id\nlf pm init --wave designer                # create the Initiative, migrate projects/tasks\nlf pm show --wave designer                # aligned live-task table\nlf pm show --wave designer --project ui   # filter to one Linear Project\nlf pm task create --wave designer --project ui --title \"Add dark mode\" --notes \"...\"\nlf pm task done --id 1207... --pr \"...\"   # close a shipped task\nlf pm sync --plan                         # show Linear/cache drift\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis PR reworks `lf pm` so the planning model maps cleanly onto Linear: a wave is an Initiative, a project is a Project, and a task is an Issue. Linear now owns project definitions and KRs in Project content; `wave/<wave>/projects/` becomes a generated offline cache and a one-time migration seed rather than a second editable source of truth. It also makes Linear OAuth durable — tokens refresh automatically before expiry using a recorded PKCE client id, so long-running waves stop dropping their connection.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **Linear model shift**: `pm.linear_project` → `pm.linear_initiative` in `GOAL.md` frontmatter; `lf pm init` creates the Initiative and migrates seeded project files into native Linear Projects.\n- **OAuth refresh**: new `token_refresh` trigger and `provider_auth` plumbing; migration `060_provider_token_oauth_client_id.sql` stores the PKCE client id. Pre-existing connections need one `lf auth linear` reconnect.\n- **Table output**: `lf pm show` renders tasks as an aligned table (new `lf/output.rs` helpers).\n- **Schema fix**: Linear project id uses the schema-correct id type.\n- **Docs/skills**: LOOPFLOW.md, README, and build/ops skills (`design`, `project-promote`, `project_clarify`, `wave_clarify`, `split-wave`, `update-wave`) updated to teach Linear-as-authoritative planning and the projects-as-cache rule; goldens refreshed for the new LOOPFLOW.md section.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 2114,
      "deletions": 738,
      "changed_files": 26
    },
    {
      "number": 866,
      "title": "wave controls: add `lf stop` and sign Mac UI tests",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf serve designer   # start the wave: one persistent loop\nlf stop designer    # stop its listener and resident gracefully\n```\n\n```bash\n# UI-test compile now builds signed runners\ncd swift && xcodegen generate\nxcodebuild build-for-testing -project LoopflowSwift.xcodeproj -scheme LoopflowMac \\\n  -destination 'platform=macOS' -disableAutomaticPackageResolution \\\n  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=YES CODE_SIGNING_REQUIRED=YES \\\n  CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Manual CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=- DEVELOPMENT_TEAM=\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nThis branch gives a served wave a graceful off switch and hardens the Mac test/release path. `lf stop <name>` posts to the listener's new lifecycle door; the listener stands down its supervisor, terminates the resident, deregisters its session, and removes this boot's discovery files. Detached worker loops are separate tmux sessions and keep running. The command is idempotent — a missing or stale endpoint is already stopped. Alongside that, Wave Chat gains a truthful stop/retry story, MessageRow renders attempt failures through a dedicated presentation model, and CI/release now compile the Mac UI test runners *signed* (ad-hoc identity) rather than with signing disabled, refusing to build an unsigned DMG.\n\n## Changes\n\n- New `Stop { name }` command wired through `lf.rs`/`lf/mod.rs`; `wave::stop` + `request_stop` talk to a new `server::ShutdownDoor` merged into the listener's graceful-shutdown select.\n- `AttemptFailurePresentation` model + tests; `MessageRow` collapses failed-badge branches and drops the unused init.\n- Wave Chat adds graceful stop and truthful retries; removes the dead `VoiceInputService` (and its tests) — ~1.8k lines deleted.\n- CI, `TESTING.md`, `swift/README.md`, and the release/screenshot scripts switch to `CODE_SIGN_IDENTITY=-` with `-disableAutomaticPackageResolution`; release refuses an ad-hoc/unnotarized user DMG. New Python gate tests keep the Mac runners signed and assert the changed-aware runner includes CI static checks.\n- Docs (`README.md`, `docs/lf.md`, `wave/README.md`) document `lf stop`; wave memory folds in the wave-controls learnings.",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 894,
      "deletions": 1947,
      "changed_files": 36
    },
    {
      "number": 867,
      "title": "lf chat: make follow mode the sole human thread surface",
      "body": "## Usage\n\n```bash\nlf chat \"ship the button audit first\"       # one-shot post into the wave's thread\nlf chat --follow -w intelligence            # watch and speak from one terminal pane\n```\n\n## Summary\n\nFolds the standalone `lf wavechat` command into `lf chat --follow`, so a single verb owns the human thread surface. One-shot `lf chat` still posts an unattributed message through the wave's door; `--follow` composes that same post door with the thread's SSE replay, giving one pane that both watches the wave's turns and speaks into the thread. Slash commands (`/status`, `/quit`, `/help`) stay local to the terminal session, and typed lines become ordinary messages (or steer requests under `--steer`).\n\n## Changes\n\n- Delete `wavechat.rs`; move its follow loop, command parsing, and status handling into `chat.rs`, sharing a single `post_message` for both one-shot and followed chat\n- Add the `--follow` flag to `lf chat` and drop the `Wavechat` command wiring in `lf.rs` and `mod.rs`\n- Update `docs/lf.md`, README, and doc comments in `sub.rs`/`thread.rs` to point at `lf chat --follow`",
      "files": [],
      "additions": 184,
      "deletions": 208,
      "changed_files": 11
    },
    {
      "number": 868,
      "title": "deps: group dependabot minor+patch, keep majors independent",
      "body": "Groups weekly minor/patch bumps into one PR per ecosystem (python/rust/swift/actions); major bumps still open individually for manual review.\n\nPairs with the merge queue now enabled on `main` so grouped bumps land themselves once CI is green.",
